\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces  Circuit for the Hadamard test to learn the inner product between two (perhaps unknown) states $\ket {\psi }$ and $\ket {\phi }$. When $b = 0$, the Hadamard test approximates from many samples $\Re (\braket {\psi }{\phi })$, and when $b = 1$, $\Im (\braket {\psi }{\phi })$ can be approximated instead. The unitary $U$ maps the state $\ket {\psi }$ to the state $\ket {\phi }$. }}{20}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hadamard_test}{{1.5}{20}{Circuit for the Hadamard test to learn the inner product between two (perhaps unknown) states $\ket {\psi }$ and $\ket {\phi }$. When $b = 0$, the Hadamard test approximates from many samples $\Re (\braket {\psi }{\phi })$, and when $b = 1$, $\Im (\braket {\psi }{\phi })$ can be approximated instead. The unitary $U$ maps the state $\ket {\psi }$ to the state $\ket {\phi }$}{figure.caption.24}{}}
