\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces  Here we consider sequences in which the state $\ket {0}$ mutates to the state $\ket {+}$. Using the closer-the-better reward scheme with $\gamma = 1/\sqrt  {2}$, panel~(a) shows the error between the heuristic \eqref  {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} and the reduced SDP \eqref  {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP}, while panel~(b) shows the runtimes of both methods. The difference between \eqref  {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP} and \eqref  {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} decreases rapidly, approaching $10^{-3}$ at $N=220$. Although there is little timing advantage for sequences up to length $80$, computing \eqref  {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} is roughly \emph  {seven} times faster than computing \eqref  {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP} for larger values of $N$. }}{74}{figure.caption.79}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ch3_QSD-beta'_beta''_pi_4_1CP}{{3.3}{74}{Here we consider sequences in which the state $\ket {0}$ mutates to the state $\ket {+}$. Using the closer-the-better reward scheme with $\gamma = 1/\sqrt {2}$, panel~(a) shows the error between the heuristic \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} and the reduced SDP \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP}, while panel~(b) shows the runtimes of both methods. The difference between \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP} and \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} decreases rapidly, approaching $10^{-3}$ at $N=220$. Although there is little timing advantage for sequences up to length $80$, computing \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} is roughly \emph {seven} times faster than computing \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP} for larger values of $N$}{figure.caption.79}{}}
